==Demographics==
{{Main|Demographics of Alessandria}}

{{See also|Demographics of Balisca}}Because of its pre-eminence as a North Aethiopic port, Alessandria has always been one of the primary gateways into [[Balisca]]. This has attracted many immigrants and made Alessandria a cosmopolitan [[W:Cultural mosaic|cultural mosaic]]. By the end of the 18th century about half the population originated from [[Illypnia]] (mostly from [[Western Illypnia]]) alongside smaller numbers from around the world.

Economic conditions and political unrest in Illypnia, [[Kaftia]] and the rest of the world brought several other waves of immigrants during the 20th century: [[Levant|Levantine]] immigrants started arriving in even larger numbers at the end of the 19th century and in the first half of the 20th century, up to 25% of the city's population was of Levantine origin; [[Sartan Baliscans|Sartans]] and [[Aetolia|Aetolians]] from 1917; <Armenians> in 1915 and 1923; {{W|Persians|Vasarasi}} and [[Indian Baliscans|Kemalis]] in the 1920s; [[Andalusian Baliscans|Andalusians]] during the 1920s and 1930s; [[Indigenous peoples in Potocsí|Indigenous Potocsinos]] after 1933; [[Central Kaftia|Central Kaftians]] (including [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayanis]], [[Imfezzan Baliscans|Imfezzans]], and [[Mozbayana|Mozbayans]]) in the inter-war period; and significant numbers of [[North Kaftia|North Kaftians]] after [[W:Decolonization|decolonization]] beginning in the aftermath of the [[Great Adonian War]].

Neighborhoods emerging as [[W:Ethnic enclave|ethnic enclaves]] include [[Sanyatunji]], [[Camerún]], [[Italia, Alessandria|Italano]] and [[Alessandria Chinatown|Chinatown]].

In 2020, in several barrios of the city, almost 70% of the young people under 18 were of non-Baliscan origin (at least one immigrant parent).
